WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.325
Lesson 1.0.

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:06.115
The Mastery Starter for Module 1, Foundations and Safety.

00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:08.147
In the next 15 minutes, we'll map the

00:00:08.147 --> 00:00:10.768
whole module: the checklist you'll use from here

00:00:10.768 --> 00:00:13.067
on, the words you'll need, and exactly how

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:14.725
you'll know you've mastered it.

00:00:16.025 --> 00:00:18.724
This module's outcome, in one line: set up

00:00:18.724 --> 00:00:21.173
and use a wallet safely, and know what

00:00:21.173 --> 00:00:24.437
can go irreversibly wrong, before any capital moves.

00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:29.152
Here's the idea this entire module is built around.

00:00:29.152 --> 00:00:32.337
Most crypto losses aren't market losses at all.

00:00:32.337 --> 00:00:35.696
They're mistakes, and scams: a seed phrase typed into

00:00:35.696 --> 00:00:38.930
a fake site, an approval signed without reading it,

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:41.532
a transfer sent on the wrong network.

00:00:41.532 --> 00:00:43.820
This module builds the specific habits

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.697
that prevent every one of those.

00:00:46.997 --> 00:00:48.287
Here's the plan.

00:00:48.287 --> 00:00:51.552
First, the 60-second version of the whole module.

00:00:51.552 --> 00:00:53.767
Second, the five words you'll need.

00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:56.804
Third, the before-signing checklist, a habit you'll

00:00:56.804 --> 00:00:59.032
use in every module from here on.

00:00:59.032 --> 00:01:01.171
And finally, what to have ready, your

00:01:01.171 --> 00:01:03.422
first safe step, and the mastery ladder.

00:01:04.722 --> 00:01:06.147
The 60-second version.

00:01:07.447 --> 00:01:10.012
Here's the whole module in one sentence.

00:01:10.012 --> 00:01:12.150
A private key that only you hold, an

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.096
approval you actually read before granting it, and

00:01:15.096 --> 00:01:18.332
a network you double-checked, prevent almost every loss

00:01:18.332 --> 00:01:20.712
that isn't simply the market moving.

00:01:20.712 --> 00:01:24.196
This module is entirely about building those three habits.

00:01:25.495 --> 00:01:26.798
It helps to separate two

00:01:26.798 --> 00:01:28.610
very different kinds of loss.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.530
A market loss is the price moving against you;

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.750
if your thesis holds, it can recover over time.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.022
A mistake loss, a wrong network, an

00:01:37.022 --> 00:01:40.240
approval signed without reading, is usually final,

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.055
with no recovery at all.

00:01:42.055 --> 00:01:43.862
This module is built entirely

00:01:43.862 --> 00:01:45.910
around preventing the second kind.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.040
Put a number on it, purely as an illustration.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.498
Someone holding $1,000 of ETH through a rough

00:01:53.498 --> 00:01:56.806
month might see it worth $800, painful, but

00:01:56.806 --> 00:02:01.015
still theirs, still recoverable if the market turns.

00:02:01.015 --> 00:02:04.881
Someone who signs one unlimited approval on a malicious

00:02:04.881 --> 00:02:07.918
app can lose the entire $1,000, in a single

00:02:07.918 --> 00:02:11.300
transaction, with nothing left to recover at all.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.615
Words you'll need.

00:02:14.915 --> 00:02:16.840
Five words for this module.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.576
A private key is the actual

00:02:18.576 --> 00:02:21.108
secret that signs your transactions.

00:02:21.108 --> 00:02:23.070
An approval is permission for an

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.213
app to move one specific token.

00:02:25.213 --> 00:02:27.811
A multisig is a wallet that needs several

00:02:27.811 --> 00:02:30.462
separate keys to agree before it acts.

00:02:30.462 --> 00:02:32.380
Phishing is a fake site or message

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.707
built specifically to steal from you.

00:02:34.707 --> 00:02:37.370
And a simulation is a preview of exactly what

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.322
a transaction will change, before you ever sign it.

00:02:41.623 --> 00:02:44.788
You'll meet each of these properly, lesson by lesson.

00:02:44.788 --> 00:02:47.378
Multisig in Lesson 1.3.

00:02:47.378 --> 00:02:49.718
Approvals in Lesson 1.4.

00:02:49.718 --> 00:02:51.883
Phishing in Lesson 1.6.

00:02:51.883 --> 00:02:54.393
And simulation in Lesson 1.7.

00:02:55.693 --> 00:02:57.648
The checklist you'll use all module.

00:02:58.948 --> 00:03:02.656
This module introduces one checklist you'll actually use for

00:03:02.656 --> 00:03:06.663
the rest of the entire program: the before-signing checklist.

00:03:06.663 --> 00:03:09.180
Seven quick checks, run every single time

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.638
before you approve or sign anything.

00:03:11.638 --> 00:03:13.895
It takes a few seconds once it's a habit,

00:03:13.895 --> 00:03:16.958
and it catches almost everything this module warns about.

00:03:18.258 --> 00:03:19.903
The first four checks.

00:03:19.903 --> 00:03:21.606
One: chain confirmed, you know

00:03:21.606 --> 00:03:23.663
exactly which network you're on.

00:03:23.663 --> 00:03:27.145
Two: the contract address, verified from an authoritative

00:03:27.145 --> 00:03:29.787
source, never just a link in a message.

00:03:29.787 --> 00:03:31.178
Three: the token and the

00:03:31.178 --> 00:03:34.092
amount, double-checked, character by character.

00:03:34.092 --> 00:03:37.467
And four: the spender, meaning the allowance, reviewed: which

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:40.352
app you're granting this permission to, and how much.

00:03:41.652 --> 00:03:44.502
Here's the anatomy of any approval, laid out.

00:03:44.502 --> 00:03:46.357
Which token the app may move.

00:03:46.357 --> 00:03:48.717
Which contract, the spender, can move it,

00:03:48.717 --> 00:03:51.377
always worth checking on a block explorer.

00:03:51.377 --> 00:03:54.369
And the amount: infinite, with no limit until

00:03:54.369 --> 00:03:57.792
revoked, or exact, covering only this one trade.

00:03:57.792 --> 00:03:59.262
Exact should be your default.

00:04:00.562 --> 00:04:02.087
The last three checks.

00:04:02.087 --> 00:04:04.916
Five: slippage, set deliberately, not left on

00:04:04.916 --> 00:04:07.492
whatever default you never looked at.

00:04:07.492 --> 00:04:09.634
Six: the gas estimate, reviewed, so

00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:12.077
nothing about the fee surprises you.

00:04:12.077 --> 00:04:15.302
And seven, the one people skip: state your intended

00:04:15.302 --> 00:04:18.517
outcome in one plain sentence, before you sign.

00:04:18.517 --> 00:04:19.633
If you can't state it

00:04:19.633 --> 00:04:20.952
simply, don't sign it yet.

00:04:22.252 --> 00:04:25.157
Notice why it's seven checks, not just one.

00:04:25.157 --> 00:04:28.238
Each check catches a different failure: the wrong

00:04:28.238 --> 00:04:30.703
chain, a spoofed address, a typo in the

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:34.524
amount, an oversized allowance, careless slippage, a surprise

00:04:34.524 --> 00:04:37.967
fee, or simply not understanding what you're doing.

00:04:37.967 --> 00:04:40.837
No single check catches all of them, which is

00:04:40.837 --> 00:04:44.207
exactly why skipping even one reopens a specific door.

00:04:45.507 --> 00:04:48.157
Here's exactly why checks two and four matter

00:04:48.157 --> 00:04:50.992
this much, a real pattern worth recognising.

00:04:50.992 --> 00:04:53.662
A popup claims to be 'verifying your wallet'.

00:04:53.662 --> 00:04:57.456
Look closer, and it's really a Permit-style signature,

00:04:57.456 --> 00:05:00.974
granting unlimited USDC access to a spender you've

00:05:00.974 --> 00:05:03.757
never seen, for the next five years.

00:05:03.757 --> 00:05:07.372
No real verification ever needs a token approval.

00:05:07.372 --> 00:05:09.174
Reject it, close the tab, and

00:05:09.174 --> 00:05:11.457
check your existing approvals instead.

00:05:12.757 --> 00:05:14.192
And here's the tool that

00:05:14.192 --> 00:05:16.502
makes check seven easy: simulation.

00:05:16.502 --> 00:05:17.916
Your wallet shows a preview of

00:05:17.916 --> 00:05:19.357
what it says will happen.

00:05:19.357 --> 00:05:21.442
A simulator runs that exact same

00:05:21.442 --> 00:05:23.827
call without actually sending it.

00:05:23.827 --> 00:05:26.730
It decodes exactly what's inside: the function,

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.147
the token, the amount, the spender.

00:05:29.147 --> 00:05:31.469
And only if all three agree with what

00:05:31.469 --> 00:05:34.402
you intended do you sign; otherwise, you reject.

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:36.747
Before you start.

00:05:38.047 --> 00:05:39.784
Before you start this module, two

00:05:39.784 --> 00:05:41.617
things should already be true.

00:05:41.617 --> 00:05:43.969
Module 0 is done: you have a secured

00:05:43.969 --> 00:05:47.274
exchange account, and a wallet whose restore you've

00:05:47.274 --> 00:05:49.672
actually tested, not just set up.

00:05:49.672 --> 00:05:51.602
And you have a small amount sitting

00:05:51.602 --> 00:05:53.586
in that wallet already, on a low-fee

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:55.302
network, ready to practise with.

00:05:56.602 --> 00:06:01.037
One more mindset worth carrying over from Lesson 0.7.

00:06:01.037 --> 00:06:04.214
Pilots train in a simulator before they ever fly

00:06:04.214 --> 00:06:07.261
a passenger: a test network, a short real hop,

00:06:07.261 --> 00:06:10.867
checking it did what was expected, then normal use.

00:06:10.867 --> 00:06:13.435
This module raises the stakes a little,

00:06:13.435 --> 00:06:16.773
multisig, hardware wallets, real approvals, so that

00:06:16.773 --> 00:06:19.662
exact discipline matters more here, not less.

00:06:20.962 --> 00:06:22.237
Your first safe step.

00:06:23.537 --> 00:06:25.486
Here's your first safe step in this

00:06:25.486 --> 00:06:27.952
module, and it takes about five minutes.

00:06:27.952 --> 00:06:30.229
Open your wallet's approvals screen,

00:06:30.229 --> 00:06:32.437
or a dedicated revocation tool.

00:06:32.437 --> 00:06:35.332
List every single approval you've ever granted.

00:06:35.332 --> 00:06:38.277
And revoke any you don't currently use, closing

00:06:38.277 --> 00:06:41.222
off standing access you probably forgot existed.

00:06:42.522 --> 00:06:45.511
If this feels familiar, it should: it's

00:06:45.511 --> 00:06:48.502
the exact habit Lesson 0.7 ended on.

00:06:48.502 --> 00:06:50.157
The difference here is scope.

00:06:50.157 --> 00:06:51.473
Last time, it was one

00:06:51.473 --> 00:06:53.627
approval, right after using it.

00:06:53.627 --> 00:06:55.862
This time, it's everything you've ever

00:06:55.862 --> 00:06:57.352
granted, in a single pass.

00:06:58.652 --> 00:06:59.747
The mastery ladder.

00:07:01.047 --> 00:07:04.082
Here's how you'll measure your progress through this module.

00:07:04.082 --> 00:07:05.197
Three rungs.

00:07:05.197 --> 00:07:07.207
Beginner: a separate burner wallet,

00:07:07.207 --> 00:07:09.294
and reading every single prompt.

00:07:09.294 --> 00:07:14.226
Practitioner: running the before-signing checklist from memory, limited approvals

00:07:14.226 --> 00:07:17.232
as the default, and a hardware wallet in use.

00:07:17.232 --> 00:07:20.665
And Master: a multisig vault, simulation on every

00:07:20.665 --> 00:07:24.784
signature, private addresses, and a tested recovery process.

00:07:26.083 --> 00:07:27.940
Notice the top rung again asks

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:29.798
for habits, not a balance.

00:07:29.798 --> 00:07:31.358
A multisig vault.

00:07:31.358 --> 00:07:34.563
Simulation, every time, with no exceptions.

00:07:34.563 --> 00:07:36.828
And a recovery process you've actually

00:07:36.828 --> 00:07:38.918
tested, not just assumed would work.

00:07:40.218 --> 00:07:42.453
Here's the first half of the road ahead.

00:07:42.453 --> 00:07:46.333
1.1, what DeFi is, and the risk-first mindset.

00:07:46.333 --> 00:07:49.769
1.2, how a transaction actually happens.

00:07:49.769 --> 00:07:53.549
1.3, wallets, keys, hardware and multisig.

00:07:53.549 --> 00:07:57.198
1.4, tokens, approvals and allowances.

00:07:57.198 --> 00:08:00.353
And 1.5, stablecoins and how they break.

00:08:01.654 --> 00:08:03.029
And the second half.

00:08:03.029 --> 00:08:05.289
1.6, scam defence.

00:08:05.289 --> 00:08:09.578
1.7, reading signatures and simulating transactions.

00:08:09.578 --> 00:08:12.783
1.8, privacy and physical security.

00:08:12.783 --> 00:08:16.143
And 1.9, smart accounts and account abstraction.

00:08:17.443 --> 00:08:20.528
And here's the whole module, recapped as one loop.

00:08:20.528 --> 00:08:23.108
Confirm the checklist before you ever sign.

00:08:23.108 --> 00:08:25.933
Limit every approval to what you actually need.

00:08:25.933 --> 00:08:29.058
Simulate first, whenever the tool is available.

00:08:29.058 --> 00:08:31.413
Revoke what you're not using, regularly.

00:08:31.413 --> 00:08:34.638
And as the amount grows, escalate to a multisig.

00:08:34.638 --> 00:08:36.420
Do those five things, and this

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:37.973
module's whole job is done.

00:08:39.273 --> 00:08:40.453
Checklist and quiz.

00:08:41.753 --> 00:08:42.963
Question one.

00:08:42.963 --> 00:08:45.267
What actually signs a transaction, your

00:08:45.267 --> 00:08:47.168
seed phrase, or your private key?

00:08:51.588 --> 00:08:53.573
The answer: your private key.

00:08:53.573 --> 00:08:55.703
Your seed phrase is what generates,

00:08:55.703 --> 00:08:57.773
and can recreate, that private key.

00:08:59.073 --> 00:09:00.318
Question two.

00:09:00.318 --> 00:09:01.919
What does a multisig wallet

00:09:01.919 --> 00:09:03.406
require before it can act?

00:09:07.826 --> 00:09:09.957
The answer: several separate keys,

00:09:09.957 --> 00:09:11.906
agreeing together, not just one.

00:09:13.206 --> 00:09:14.476
Question three.

00:09:14.476 --> 00:09:16.521
What does a simulation actually show you?

00:09:20.941 --> 00:09:23.276
The answer: a preview of exactly what a

00:09:23.276 --> 00:09:26.136
transaction will change, before you ever sign it.

00:09:27.436 --> 00:09:28.691
Question four.

00:09:28.691 --> 00:09:30.299
Why is an exact approval

00:09:30.299 --> 00:09:32.101
safer than an unlimited one?

00:09:36.521 --> 00:09:38.655
The answer: it caps exactly what an

00:09:38.655 --> 00:09:40.670
app can ever move, no matter what

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:42.211
happens to that app later.

00:09:43.511 --> 00:09:44.826
Question five.

00:09:44.826 --> 00:09:46.440
What's the first safe step this

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:47.651
module asks you to take?

00:09:52.071 --> 00:09:54.320
The answer: open your approvals, and

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:56.082
revoke any you no longer use.

00:09:57.382 --> 00:09:59.886
You've mastered this module when you can

00:09:59.886 --> 00:10:02.207
take a brand-new app from first visit

00:10:02.207 --> 00:10:05.317
to a limited, simulated, verified transaction.

00:10:05.317 --> 00:10:07.637
And revoke the approval afterward, without

00:10:07.637 --> 00:10:09.147
needing to look anything up.

00:10:10.447 --> 00:10:14.375
This is education, not financial advice, and every dollar

00:10:14.375 --> 00:10:18.332
figure or hypothetical in this module is illustrative.

00:10:18.332 --> 00:10:21.014
Nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:10:21.014 --> 00:10:24.071
your seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:10:25.371 --> 00:10:28.820
That's your Mastery Starter for Module one, Foundations

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:32.453
and Safety: the before-signing checklist, five words, your

00:10:32.453 --> 00:10:35.216
first safe step, and the mastery ladder.

00:10:35.216 --> 00:10:37.703
Next, Lesson one point one: what DeFi

00:10:37.703 --> 00:10:40.649
actually is, and the risk-first mindset that

00:10:40.649 --> 00:10:43.071
runs through everything from here on.
